  "sent_to_model": "I'd like help writing a first-person poem in rhyming verse. It's about someone born into a rich family who becomes unhappy with that life and finds purpose helping people in poorer neighbourhoods. At graduation they give up their share of the family business and decide to become a mentor in Englewood. Please include their father's objection and their reply that helping people is their own choice, unlike the wealth they inherited. I want to follow through on the cost: donating the money, depending on welfare and losing contact with family, while still believing they made the right choice.\n\n- Narrator later depends on welfare.\n- Father offers half a billion dollars, a family business stake and a Lambo.\n- Helping includes shelter construction and housing young people.\n- No family contact after departure.\n- Englewood is chosen for living and mentoring.\n- Graduation is the occasion for renouncing the business stake.\n- Encounters with poor people on South Side and West.\n- Narrator donates the money.\n- Helping brings pride and satisfaction, without pay.\n- Father regards leaving as ruining narrator's life.\n- Family's preoccupations: property, money and social standing.\n- Luxury vehicles, planes and status hold little appeal.\n- Distance from family grows into anger.\n- Narrator feels out of place at family social events.\n- Narrator believes the departure was worthwhile.\n\n(About me: poet. Length: it should come to roughly 250-400 words.)",
  "hidden": {
    "must_include": [],
    "must_cover": [
      "Write a first-person poem in rhyming verse.",
      "Show growing alienation from inherited wealth and purpose found in helping poorer people.",
      "At graduation the narrator gives up the family business stake and decides to mentor in Englewood.",
      "Include the father's objection and the narrator's reply distinguishing inherited wealth from their own choice to help others.",
      "Show the donation of the money, welfare dependence and loss of family contact, alongside continued belief in the decision."
    ],
